  "summary": "Traditional Japanese storehouses built with fireproof and secure construction techniques.",
  "description": "Characterized by thick earthen walls (dojo), tiled roofs, and white-plastered exteriors with distinctive black beams. Developed during Edo period to protect valuable goods from fire and theft, featuring heavy construction with minimal windows and reinforced doors.",
  "culturalContext": "The kura (dozou) — Japanese fireproof storehouses — protected merchant wealth through Edo-period fires: thick mud-and-plaster walls over timber frames (dozozukuri), tiny barred windows, double-plate metal doors, whitewashed or blackened plaster surfaces, and stepped gables (shireba) whose heights signaled the owner's fortune. Kurashiki's canal-side kura quarter and Hakodate's warehouses preserve the type; storehouse aesthetics (compactness, dark shutters, plaster patina) remain the image of Japanese mercantile prudence.",
